The real power of Spring's stereotype model is that you can **compose your own**. Any annotation that is itself annotated with `@Component` (or any of its specializations) becomes a stereotype that component-scan discovers.

**Why this matters for architecture**
- Encodes architecture rules in code instead of in developer memory
- Instead of relying on developers to remember 'use `@Service` for business logic and `@Repository` for data access', you create domain-specific stereotypes that carry those rules
- Custom stereotypes can also compose runtime behavior — `@Transactional`, custom AOP, validation

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Service
@Transactional
public @interface UseCase {}

@UseCase
public class PlaceOrderUseCase {
    public Order execute(OrderRequest request) { ... }
}
```

**What this enables**
- The `@UseCase` annotation is discovered by component-scan (inherits from `@Service` which inherits from `@Component`)
- Every class annotated with it automatically gets `@Transactional` behavior
- Common in **hexagonal architecture** codebases where the port/adapter boundary is enforced through custom stereotypes rather than package naming conventions

**Other common custom stereotypes**
- `@DomainService` — business-logic class with custom auditing aspect
- `@QueryHandler` / `@CommandHandler` — CQRS pattern with auto-registration in a dispatcher
- `@Adapter` — hexagonal architecture infrastructure layer with logging aspect
